js(V/'assumption-guarantee-matrix.json',rows);write(V/'assumption-guarantee-matrix.md','# Assumption/guarantee graph\n\n| ID | Owner | Role | Interval | Dependents | Assumption |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n'+'\n'.join(f"| {a['id']} | {a['owner']} | {a['role']} | {a['interval']} | {', '.join(a['dependent_properties'])} | {a['guarantee']} |" for a in rows)+'\n\nNo global minimality claim follows from individual perturbations. Authentication of a canonical source state does not establish issuer solvency or source EVM implementation correctness.\n')
write(V/'assumptions.md','# Assumptions\n\nSee assumption-guarantee-matrix.json for ownership, role, lifetime and dependents. Amounts are integers in separate security/cash domains. A height advances through ledger progress only. All modeled domains are finite except deliberately exposed duplicate-effect counters, which are checked before a second payout. No production contact, credentials, assets or paid infrastructure are involved.\n\nFairness is an environmental premise, never an init assertion. For every trade t, use WF(advanceDecisionHeight), WF(abort(t)), WF(capture(t,phase)), WF(prove(t,phase)), WF(deliver(t,phase)), WF(apply(t,leg)), WF(reconcile(t)). These are justified only after eventual stable recovery of the relevant actors, retained data and submission authority. They guarantee neither a time bound nor COMMIT before D.\n')
write(V/'threat-model.md','# Threat model\n\nUntrusted relayers may delay, drop, replay, fabricate or substitute evidence. A certificate must authenticate immutable finalized provenance and every descriptor/public binding. Outages and crash/restart do not rewrite history. Byzantine source quorum, arbitrary issuer destruction, verifier takeover, irreversible evidence loss and key compromise are explicit assumption perturbations. Transport, custody, validator, upgrade and disclosure keys are independent authorities. Buyer/seller identity is independent of institution hosting their eligible accounts.\n')
write(V/'protocol.md','# Versioned candidate and model boundary\n\nCandidate v0 is the shared answer saved in sources/shared-solution.original-ru.md. Candidate v1 is the complete self-contained reference in sources/task-v2.md sections 1–2: decision on A, native durable holds, exclusive height deadline and abort tombstones. No architecture substitution is accepted as a repair.\n\nA atomically registers OPEN with its security reservation. B may prepare first without inspecting live A. A commits only using a received authentic finalized B reservation proof, local hold and height < D; recording COMMIT and transferring security is one local transaction and must roll back together. After height >= D, a signed complete descriptor can establish ABORT even without OPEN. Refund is a separately retryable application. B stores authenticated terminal knowledge even before its hold exists; late preparation after unknown ABORT remains resolvable.\n\nHistorical authentic proofs remain authentic after the source changes. Their safety depends on durable obligation semantics and terminal knowledge. Network messages do not authorize financial effects without verification. COMPLETE reporting requires both local application evidence.\n\nThe model separates ledgers and messages. Each actor action reads only its local state and received evidence. Specifications may inspect the combined world. A small explicit message soup is selected for the compact ledger overlay; framework scaffolding would obscure the local transaction/read boundaries. Upstream skill sign-off suggestions are superseded by the user instruction to proceed without routine confirmation.\n\nC-Decision is irreversible backed commitment; C-Local concerns actual local economic application/usability; C-Records concerns evidence-supported reporting. The asynchronous candidate permits unilateral local usability after COMMIT and before remote application.\n')
write(C/'proposal.md','# Proposal\n\n## Why\n\nThe proposed cross-chain DvP protocol has no executed formal evidence, and its atomicity wording can be stronger than its asynchronous application guarantee. Establish reproducible evidence without assuming correctness.\n\n## What Changes\n\n- Build and run layered Quint models, counterexample controls and bounded/finite verification.\n- Trace every mandatory v2 suite, including variants, privacy, timing and operational integration.\n- Preserve exact source identity, toolchain, replayable traces and external obligations.\n\n## Capabilities\n\n### New Capabilities\n\n- `dvp-verification`: Evidence-backed protocol investigation and scoped guarantee reporting.\n\n### Modified Capabilities\n\nNone; this is a new repository.\n\n## Impact\n\nProject-local Quint and model-checker dependencies, verification artifacts and workflow. No real banking, assets, credentials, deployment or paid infrastructure.\n')
write(C/'design.md','# Design\n\nUse a finite native-hold core plus evidence, operational/control, relational privacy and timed resource models. Reuse descriptors and economic contracts across A/B/D variants. Preserve every counterexample before any repair. Run smoke controls first, then core witnesses/simulation/bounded/finite/temporal checks, then required variants, trust, privacy, timing and integrated configurations.\n\nModels are abstractions; only checked mappings establish refinement, and safety mappings do not preserve liveness automatically. Candidate sources and model/tool revisions are identified in verification/sources and results/manifest.json. Full v2 scope remains NOT DONE until every mandatory suite has an executed result or a precise visible blocker.\n')
write(C/'tasks.md','# Tasks\n\n- [x] 1. Read attached v2 and shared solution; preserve source identity and limitations.\n- [x] 2. Create property, assumption, coverage and variant contracts before modeling.\n- [ ] 3. Validate toolchain: true/false safety and fair/unfair temporal smoke models.\n- [ ] 4. Implement core, deterministic witnesses, simulation, Apalache and TLC checks.\n- [ ] 5. Kill at least eight isolated protocol mutants; preserve and replay traces.\n- [ ] 6. Execute required topology, reservation and compact mechanism variants.\n- [ ] 7. Execute operational recovery, membership, intervention and trust perturbations.\n- [ ] 8. Execute observer access, disclosure, relational privacy and leak controls.\n- [ ] 9. Execute asynchronous/eventual/bounded timing, queue/capacity and negative controls.\n- [ ] 10. Execute small integrated/privacy-time and multilateral configurations; audit mappings.\n- [ ] 11. Reconcile literal coverage, conformance obligations, findings and final reports.\n- [ ] 12. Complete relevant workflow/spec checks and publish local reproducible evidence.\n\n## Resume\n\nInitial sources and contracts exist. Next: complete toolchain smoke, then core model. Original source PDFs/workspace are absent from requested local source locations; the shared response was recovered from embedded public page data, not another chat API.\n')
